=== abstract
An evaluator can often afford to run a new model on only $k$ of the $N$ benchmarks a leaderboard holds. We ask which $k$ to run, how to infer the rest, and how large $k$ must be, on 238 embedding models and 328 MTEB tasks with whole organisations held out and every method scored on benchmarks that no selection rule can choose. A probabilistic factor model with a greedy design criterion beats random selection by about 1.9 points of RMSE when two or three benchmarks are run and, at a margin of a quarter of the floor, is equivalent to it from about eight; no other inference rule we tried beats the factor model by more than that margin. At eight benchmarks the RMSE is within 2 points of what the model's other scores give, at 32 to 50 within 1 point, and no $k$ we tried resolves neighbouring models: the floor is about 5 points, 2.5 times what the factor model predicts, and we do not find why. The closed-form risk of the model explains the design part of the error but not the floor, so it is a decomposition and not a rule for choosing $k$.
=== experiments
\section{Results}
\label{sec:experiments}

All results are on the common target set of Section~\ref{sec:protocol}, with the factor model (P5) for inference unless a rule is named. The headline subset is the 154 held-out models, from 51 organisations, that can be given 109 benchmarks from the pool (and have at least 37 observed target benchmarks); they are the same at every $k$. The temporal and strength splits have 13 models (4 organisations) and 22 models (11 organisations) in that subset, so their intervals are indicative only (Appendix~\ref{app:data}). Intervals are 90\% group-bootstrap intervals. Selection rules are S0 (random), S1 (the benchmarks with the most scores among the training models), S2 (the benchmarks with the largest standard deviation, in points, among the training models), S4 (pivoted QR on the training matrix completed at the fitted rank, a column-subset-selection baseline~\citep{boutsidis2009css}) and S6 (Section~\ref{sec:method}); the simple inference baselines are P0 (the benchmark mean, which ignores the revealed scores) and P1 (the mean of the revealed standardised scores for every benchmark).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figtab/fig1_error_vs_k.pdf}
\caption{Error of a new model's scores on the common target set $T$ against the number of benchmarks run $k$ (154 held-out models, 51 organisations; 50 C/T splits; $|T|=109$ of $N=328$). (a) RMSE in points for designed selection (S6, factor-model inference), random selection (S0) with the factor model and with mean-offset inference, and the benchmark mean; the floor is the same model's error with all of its observed pool benchmarks given. (b) Excess over the floor, with the pre-set margins $\delta$ and $2\delta$ ($\approx0.1$ and $0.2$ points) and the secondary margins $0.25$ and $0.5$ of the floor as horizontal lines; $k^*(\epsilon)$ is where the curve crosses a line (white diamond and bar: empirical $k^*$ and its 5th--95th bootstrap percentiles on the grid); dashed: the prediction of Section~\ref{sec:theory}. (c) Paired gain of S6 over S0 with its 90\% interval and the margins; the dashed curve is the predicted gain, and the yellow curve the best of S1, S2 and S4 at each $k$, chosen after seeing the gains.}
\label{fig:error_vs_k}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Structure.}
One general factor dominates: the leading eigenvalue of the correlation matrix of the standardised scores carries 63\% of the variance and the second 13\% (Figure~\ref{fig:spectrum}a, Appendix~\ref{app:further}). Beyond that the spectrum decays slowly and the rank estimators we set out before the results disagree (parallel analysis 5 to 10, held-out cross-validation about 16, Gavish--Donoho 56; Figure~\ref{fig:spectrum}b). We make no claim about a number of factors; the rank used for inference is chosen by inner cross-validation at each $k$.

